  "name": "RDP (Remote Desktop Protocol) from the Internet",
  "tags": [
    "Tactic: Command and Control",
    "Domain: Endpoint",
    "Use Case: Threat Detection",
    "Data Source: PAN-OS",
    "Resources: Investigation Guide"
  ],
  "interval": "5m",
  "enabled": true,
  "revision": 2,
  "description": "Detects network traffic from an external system to an internal system on Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP), normally TCP port 3389. This shows that access was attempted or observed; it does not prove that someone logged in or controlled the computer.",
  "risk_score": 47,
  "severity": "medium",
  "note": "Investigation\n\n1. Check the time, source address, destination address, port, firewall action, and whether the connection was allowed.\n2. Confirm whether the source is an approved VPN, supplier, or support address and whether the destination should provide RDP.\n3. Check the destination computer for successful and failed RDP logins around the alert time.\n4. Review related firewall, threat, and endpoint alerts.\n\nResponse\n\nDo not automatically block or isolate the system from this alert alone. Confirm the activity with the system owner. Escalate unexplained or unauthorised access, or any confirmed successful login, through the approved incident-response process.",
  "license": "Elastic License v2",
  "output_index": "",
  "timeline_id": "300afc76-072d-4261-864d-4149714bf3f1",
  "timeline_title": "Comprehensive Network Timeline",
  "timestamp_override": "event.ingested",
  "author": [
    "Elastic"
  ],
  "false_positives": [
    "Some network security policies allow RDP directly from the Internet but usage that is unfamiliar to server or network owners can be unexpected and suspicious. RDP services may be exposed directly to the Internet in some networks such as cloud environments. In such cases, only RDP gateways, bastions or jump servers may be expected expose RDP directly to the Internet and can be exempted from this rule. RDP may be required by some work-flows such as remote access and support for specialized software products and servers. Such work-flows are usually known and not unexpected."
  ],
